<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/expert/investigative-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Investigative journalism is the profession\'s <strong>long-form verification discipline</strong>
    — months of documents, sources and cross-checking aimed at what powerful people prefer
    unreported. It is this ladder\'s capstone: every method lesson above, at the highest stakes
    the craft knows.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="documents, sources, bulletproofing, impact" widget="equationStep" config={invCfg} />

  <h2>The masterclass cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Watergate (1972–74):</strong> a burglary followed to a presidency — source cultivation (Deep Throat), document trails, editorial courage; the case that defined investigative journalism\'s civic role and its legal protections (Sullivan-era law, again the law lesson).</li>
    <li><strong>Spotlight (2002):</strong> church records mined as a database — the pattern invisible in single cases, undeniable in aggregate: investigative + data journalism converging (the previous lesson\'s pipeline, sanctified by outcome).</li>
    <li><strong>Offshore-leaks era (2014–):</strong> ICIJ\'s collaborative model replaced the lone-scoop myth — hundreds of newsrooms, shared documents, simultaneous publication (jurisdictional safety in numbers): the distributed lesson, accountable edition.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the ladder and the atlas end:</strong> investigation is the professional form of this atlas\'s core habit — check the primary source, name the base rate, show the method. A citizen who has walked these 39 ladders reads the news the way an editor does: that was always the point.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Woodward & Bernstein, All the President\'s Men (1974); the Spotlight Team\'s methodology (Boston Globe, 2002); ICIJ collaboration models; the verification and cryptography lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
